            <td class="p-3">Higher Rate</td>
            <td class="p-3">£50,271 to £125,140</td>
            <td class="p-3 font-bold text-amber-600 dark:text-amber-400">40%</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3">Additional Rate</td>
            <td class="p-3">Above £125,140</td>
            <td class="p-3 font-bold text-red-600 dark:text-red-400">45%</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>
      Let's look at a concrete example to demystify this. If you earn £60,000 a year, you do <strong>not</strong> pay 40% on the entire £60,000. Instead, you pay 0% on the first £12,570, 20% on the amount between £12,571 and £50,270 (£37,700 taxed at 20% = £7,540), and 40% <em>only</em> on the remaining £9,730 that falls into the Higher Rate band (£9,730 taxed at 40% = £3,892). This marginal structure is very often misunderstood, causing unnecessary panic when an employee receives a pay rise that ostensibly "pushes them into the next tax bracket." Your overall effective tax rate is always lower than your highest marginal tax rate.
    </p>
    
    <p>
      You can effortlessly test different salary figures, bonus payouts, and scenarios using our free <a href="/countries/united-kingdom/income-tax/income-tax-calculator/" class="text-link hover:underline dark:text-link font-semibold">UK Income Tax Calculator</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="personal-allowance-taper">2. Personal Allowance & The £100,000 Tax Trap (60% Marginal Rate)</h2>
    <p>
      Standard UK taxpayers receive an annual tax-free <a href="/knowledge/glossary/uk-personal-allowance/" class="text-link hover:underline dark:text-link">Personal Allowance</a> of £12,570. This acts as a foundation of tax-free earning to support basic living costs before HMRC takes a cut. However, high earners face a significant cliff edge known notoriously as the <strong>£100,000 Personal Allowance Taper</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      As your 'adjusted net income' exceeds £100,000, HMRC begins to aggressively claw back this allowance. It does so at a strict rate of £1 for every £2 earned above the £100k threshold. This means that by the time your income reaches exactly £125,140, your entire £12,570 Personal Allowance has been wiped out completely.
    </p>

    <div class="rounded-xl border-l-4 border-amber-500 bg-amber-50/50 p-4 dark:bg-amber-950/30 my-6">
      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200 m-0">Warning: The 60% Marginal Tax Rate</h3>
      <p class="text-amber-800 dark:text-amber-300 mt-2 mb-0">
        Between £100,000 and £125,140, this loss of tax-free allowance coupled with the standard 40% Higher Rate tax results in an effective <strong>60% marginal tax rate</strong>. If we factor in the additional 2% National Insurance (which continues to apply at this level), you are effectively keeping only 38 pence of every £1 you earn in this income window! This is widely considered one of the harshest quirks of the UK tax system, serving as a severe disincentive for earning between these amounts. Fortunately, it can be mitigated with clever pension planning, which we cover below.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2 id="national-insurance">3. Class 1 National Insurance Contributions: The Hidden Tax</h2>
    <p>
      Income tax is only one part of the take-home pay equation. Employees also pay Class 1 <a href="/knowledge/glossary/uk-national-insurance/" class="text-link hover:underline dark:text-link">National Insurance (NI)</a> through the <a href="/knowledge/glossary/uk-paye/" class="text-link hover:underline dark:text-link">PAYE (Pay As You Earn) system</a>. National Insurance was originally designed to fund state benefits, including the State Pension, statutory sick pay, and maternity leave, though today it essentially acts as a secondary income tax.
    </p>
    <p>
      The rates for employees have fluctuated notably in recent years due to successive budget policy shifts, but as of the current legislation for 2025/2026, the structure is as follows:
    </p>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 mb-6">
      <li><strong>Main Rate (8%):</strong> Deducted on weekly earnings between the Primary Threshold (£242 per week or £12,570 per year) and the Upper Earnings Limit (£967 per week or £50,270 per year).</li>
      <li><strong>Upper Rate (2%):</strong> Deducted on all earnings above £50,270 per year.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Crucially, unlike Income Tax, which is calculated on an annual cumulative basis across the whole tax year, National Insurance is calculated purely on a per-pay-period basis (e.g., weekly or monthly). This means that a sudden, large bonus in one specific month could see a spike in your NI contributions for that specific month, which doesn't get balanced out over the rest of the year.
    </p>

